<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Milovan Djilas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1911">1911</a>–<a href="/years/1995">1995</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Milovan Đilas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Milovan Djilas was a Yugoslav communist politician, theorist and author, born near Mojkovac in the Kingdom of Montenegro. Jailed and tortured as a student radical in the 1930s, he joined the politburo of the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> in 1939, helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Josip Broz Tito</a> build the Partisan resistance in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and became vice-president in Tito's government. Once regarded as Tito's possible successor, he was expelled from the Central Committee in January 1954 after articles arguing against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a>. In The New Class, published abroad in 1957, he argued that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> had created a privileged class of party bureaucracy, and he spent years in prison for his writings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/milovan-djilas.jpg" alt="Milovan Djilas" width="330" height="451" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stevan Kragujević · CC BY-SA 3.0 rs · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stevan_Kragujevic,_Milovan_Djilas,1950.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Milovan Djilas</b>, once among the leaders of communist Yugoslavia, argued in The New Class (1957) that the party bureaucracy had become a new privileged ruling class, and his prison sentence was lengthened for publishing the book abroad.</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · Weaknesses</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Podbišće, in what is now <a href="/countries/montenegro">Montenegro</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.94,19.57&amp;year=1965" aria-label="Podbišće on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5023 639 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Milovan Djilas"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5473 909h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5473 909h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.94,19.57&amp;year=1965">What was here in 1965? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
